(1052, 2, '2014-09-01 17:21:40', '0000-00-00 00:00:00', '<p>My initial interest in ritual was fueled by a series of blogposts on LessWrong.com, a community blog about rationality. I\'m going to summarize a several of them here, building off them later.</p><p> </p><p><a href=\"http://lesswrong.com/lw/by9/to_like_each_other_sing_and_dance_in_synchrony/\">To Like Each Other, Sing and Dance in Unison</a></p><blockquote><p>Throughout most cultures, singing and dancing together (and other synchronous actions) fosters trust and in-group cohesion, making them more likely to help each other.</p></blockquote><p><a href=\"http://lesswrong.com/lw/kov/roles_are_martial_arts_for_agency/\">Roles are Martial Arts for Agency</a></p><blockquote><p>Martial arts isn\'t about learning the right way to throw a punch or the best way to block. The entire purpose of martial arts is to train your body to react with minimal conscious deliberation, to remove “you” from the equation as much as possible. Conscious thought is too slow. If you have to think about what you’re doing, you’ve already lost.</p><p> </p><p>Likewise, when one is well-trained in a certain Role, one defaults to certain prescribed actions immediately and confidently. I’ve acted as a guy standing around watching people faint in an overcrowded room, and I’ve acted as the guy telling people to clear the area. The difference was in one I had the role of Corporate Pleb, and the other I had the role of Guy Responsible For Shit.</p><p> </p><p>Deliberate, rational agency can help you decide what Role to choose, but it often won\'t be fast enough to make decisions on the fly, when something goes wrong.</p></blockquote><p> </p>', 'Links from Less Wrong', '', 'draft', 'open', 'open', '', '', '', '', '2014-09-01 17:21:40', '0000-00-00 00:00:00', '', 0, 'http://humanistculture.com/?p=1052', 0, 'post', '', 0);
